<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Hardened-runtime entitlements for the notarized Developer ID build.

      DELIBERATELY EMPTY. XDownloader is a NON-sandboxed Developer ID app
      (distributed outside the Mac App Store), and under the hardened runtime it
      needs no entitlement for what it actually does:

        • Spawning yt-dlp / gallery-dl / ffmpeg via Process() — each runs as a
          SEPARATE child process (posix_spawn/exec), which the hardened runtime
          does not restrict. Library validation only governs code loaded INTO
          our own process, not children we launch.
        • Outbound network — not sandboxed, so com.apple.security.network.client
          is a sandbox entitlement we do not need.

      We intentionally DO NOT add com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation:
      the only embedded third-party code is Sparkle.framework, and scripts/
      sign-notarize.sh re-signs Sparkle (and its nested XPC services, Autoupdate,
      and Updater.app) with OUR Developer ID (team 5KGJ4RW2K5). Because every
      dylib we load is then signed by our team, default library validation passes
      untouched — disabling it would only weaken the app for no benefit.

      Nor do we add allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory / DYLD-env
      exceptions: the app runs no JIT and loads no unsigned code.

      If a future feature genuinely requires an entitlement, add ONLY that one
      here with a comment justifying it — do not blanket-add exceptions.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
